 mobility  strategies and their responses  in fact we currently help with more than  60 crises each year such as hurricanes  and earthquakes  and we're helping cities get safer  through beacons by giving drivers a way  to stay connected even when they're out  of range of gps  in our newest initiative waze carpool is  helping to combat  the rising congestion in cities around  the world  our city partnerships and community  driven approach is what makes waze  unique  and sets us apart from other navigation  apps if you're interested in learning  more about waze or if you're interested  in becoming a ways for cities partner  visit waze.com ways for cities  now through our waste for cities program  we offer a variety of tools and  offerings which include  data sharing carpool beacons and crisis  mitigation  today we're going to focus on how google  cloud has helped us  improve one of these offerings the data  that we share with partners  through our data sharing offering waze  provides real-time proprietary incident  and slow down information generally  generated by millions of ways drivers to  inform our partners mobility strategies  the data we share includes user  generated reports like traffic jams  crashes constructions and potholes just  to name a few  now before we begin working with google  cloud waze data was shared with our city  partners via a geo rss feed  in json and xml formats partners had to  take a lot of steps to begin using this  data  these steps included ingesting storing  and then paying for the ongoing storage  of this waze data  among several other technical details  needless to say it was really difficult  for many of our resource straps city  partners to consider this process  which really limited most of our  partners ability to use our data at all  so that's why we turn to google cloud to  find a way to manage and share this data  with our partners in a simpler and  overall more cost effective way  now i'll turn it over to my colleague  michael to talk about the technical  implementation of this tool  okay hi so starting with the  waze and uh google cloud platform  integration so obviously bigquery  we started with bigquery and in  combination with data studio  was the um platform or the services of  choice  okay challenges so um  first we need to ingest data from an  outside source  so waze provided us an api to hit and we  we use that api to pull in data all the  world's traffic data  another challenge is that uh we the  system had to support  thousands of partners at once so there's  no telling when partners were using the  system or making queries  so therefore we had to assume that there  would be a lot of concurrency in the  thousands  um simply user-aware dashboards  meaning that if i'm a partner and i go  to go to a dashboard  i would like to see the data that's  relevant to me whereas  another partner should see the data  that's relevant to them without moving  around  um or having to to set that on every  login  so that was really important another  challenge would be if i were to make  changes to the default dashboards  they should they should propagate for  all partners so i didn't want to have to  uh edit or have individual dashboards  per partner  it was a request or does not pay model  meaning that  partners are not charged for this  service and that that  google or waze in this case is is  providing the service for free  and then one of the major challenges  which wasn't technical is that it was a  small development team with little to no  sre support  okay before i go into details on the  technical architecture i want to call  out a teammate  igan sores from our laptop customer  engineering team  who really helped design and develop  these pipelines  i myself worked with egon but also  mainly focused on the  connector and the dashboard development  so uh number one we had our data  pipelines  so we ingested the world's um traffic  data  in essence into bigquery uh number two  user management  partners on board into the system  through a simple web portal  and then number three they go to a  default dashboard  they get access to the data that's of  interest to them and number four is  like how to use the partner's behavior  how the partners are actually accessing  the data to make judgments on  what data is valuable to our partners  and what dashboards are they  are they really finding value in so we  can modify the dashboards  accordingly okay the data pipeline  we used app engine flex and its chrome  functionality  to stream data in from waze's  traffic api into bigquery partition  tables  and so cron would run periodically and  we would basically  query the api and pull in all the data  for that period since the last  run and write it into partition tables  and then  from these partition tables we would set  up we set up bigquery schedule  jobs and so what they would do is they  would transform and dedupe the data  writing into writing into cluster tables  in bigquery  okay user management the u  the user management consisted of  partners  um logging into a web portal  and and selecting the polygons they're  interested in  and and this gets written into a partner  table in bigquery we chose to use  bigquery because  just for simplicity it's not i mean this  is a very small table so it doesn't  really fit the bigquery use case  but uh for simplicity we just decided to  keep the user information  in bigquery as well so uh similar  following a similar pattern as the data  pipelines we have an  app engine job that runs in that uses  cron to run periodically  it looks at the partner table and based  on changes whether a partner gets added  or removed  it creates spec it creates authorized  views  for these partners in bigquery now you  see  uh as you see from the diagram there's  there's something called  intermediate views and we had to add  these because of a  uh to work around the 2500  uh limit of authorized views per data  set so  in essence it's a we have created views  of views  and and intermediate views basically use  an ascii regex to dock root partners in  in different intermediate views and this  allowed us to get over that 2500 limit  and now minhas is going to cover the  reporting requirements  thank you michael stepping from the  technical challenges we had  uh we determined what the recur  requirements for the reporting would be  first was that the data for separate  cities were in separate data sets  they would have to be accessed from  separate data sets and there we'd have  to ensure  data privacy because uh we didn't want  any of the partners to see  other partners data so if they were  looking at a dashboard or any kind of  reporting solution  they would only be able to see their own  cities data and not anyone else's one  there is also a requirement for the  centralized billing to have that  frictionless user experience  because since we didn't want the  city's partners to carry the bill for  this dashboard  the billing would be meant managed  centrally by the  ways and gcp teams so there will be a  central billing account that will be  taking care of the bigquery querying  through the dashboard  there also have to be a single point of  change for all the dashboard edits  uh that means if a future change was  made to the dashboard or if any kind of  update was to be made  it would have to get rolled out to all  the partners at the same time  so there have to be some kind of a easy  deployment system for that  and based on these requirements uh we  decided to use data studio  data studio is a data visualization and  reporting  tool that's available freely from google  marketing platform  and it works very well with bigquery it  has a native connector to bigquery  if you're using bigquery data in data  studio you can use the bi engine to take  advantage of its fast in-memory uh  calculations and  get very speedy results so when  as the team was looking into this  solution  we realized that there were several  alternatives available  first was using the native connector to  create a single dashboard with viewers  credentials  what vious credentials mean is that when  someone would view the dashboard  their credentials would get passed  through data studio into the data source  which here would be bigquery and then  that  credentials would be used to enforce  what data they would get to see and here  would be using  [Music]  row level access either through data  studios  email based filtering or bigquery's  current user function but the limitation  here was that if we wanted to use either  of those two features we'd have to  merge all those datasets into one single  uh  table or source and then filter from  there which became a little bit  complicated because there were  over 1400 partners the second option was  to use again the native connector but  there would  create one template dashboard and then  copy over the dashboard  multiple times like each time one time  for each partner and then edit the  data source that it would point to the  right data set  that had its own challenges because it  would be possible to copy over the  dashboards  but if we wanted to make any future  changes or wanted to do maintenance  doing it over 1400 different dashboards  would become  near impossible so we opted for the  third solution which was the custom  connector  and we'll go into that more details  later but what the custom connector  would do is it would  make a customized solution involving  data studio and bigquery  it would meet all the repo reporting  requirements that i just mentioned  earlier  and this would require very minimum  level of development from the  team that was working on it the custom  connectors are built  on the community connector plat uh  platform for data  studio these are written in apps script  which are which is  uh google's cloud-based server-less  scripting engine and it's it takes very  low amount of effort to build  connectors using this framework anyone  can build a connector  you can connect to any external data  sources and pull in the data into data  studio  and it's also possible to share and  publish those connectors so that others  can use  if you are interested in learning more  about these connectors you can  check out the codelab we have at this  url  on the screen the comedy characters is  already a established program where  there are over 200 published connectors  available  and here at google we ourselves have  used this platform to  create several connectors to serve data  to  the public for example the chrome ux  connector  uses this connector platform to serve  chrome ux report data to users  so it was already a proven solution for  us however  one thing we have never tried with these  solutions was that  we didn't implement the your user base  view  in any of these connectors so what that  means is  in user base view multiple users will  look at the same dashboard but based on  their credentials who they are  it would fetch data from different data  sets which is a bit different from row  level access because we are accessing  completely different data sets and then  the dashboard would get populated with  data from those data sets  so this was something new that the team  was trying out uh with the community  character  approach and our end result would look  something similar to this that the  connector would get created and  using that connector we'd create a  dashboard a single dashboard link and  that would be sent to all the 400  different partners  when a partner opens their dashboard  link they will see their dashboard with  their own data  and they wouldn't have any flexibility  or option to switch over to see  a separate user dashboard so this is a  complete user experience we wanted to  have  user gets a link clicks on it they  immediately view their report  so to do this under the hood this is  what we had to implement  so once the user clicks on the dashboard  link it opens up the data studio  dashboard  and the data studio data studio will  then execute  the connector with viewers credentials  the first time they use any user opens  this dashboard they will have to go  through an authorization flow where they  authorized it uh this connector to run  on their behalf  but this is done only for the first time  they use it  for all subsequent times the dashboard  will open up automatically  so data studio executes the connector  script and the connector script then  takes the user's friendships and figures  out all right this is the email for the  this user who's viewing the dashboard  and then it makes a call to the bigquery  rest api  to figure out which dash which data set  should  uh this user be viewing so we had a  mapping table that mapped each user's  email address  with a specific list of data sets so  this rest api call enabled us  to query into bigquery and get the  result back where  bigquery would say all right this email  address x has  access to data set y so once this  information was  back in the connector we then construct  a new  query and this query string would  contain basically this  data set id that we just fetched so it  would say something like  select asterisk from data set y but we  would not send this query off to  bigquery directly what we would do is  that  then the connector would use the  bigquery uh the data studio advanced  services to make the query directly into  bigquery  so let's look a bit deeper into that  what that means  uh to communicate between data studio  and bigquery the native connector  directly works but when we're  working with a community character the  data will usually  have to pass through apps script and it  will have to be transformed and  processed  in abstract which adds a bit of  overhead in calculation and latency  using  advanced services for data studio what  we can do is rather than  passing the data through apps script we  can just pass the query itself to data  studio  and the data studio will make pass that  query to bigquery directly and fetch the  data  from bigquery using a native connection  the data does not pass through  apps script in any way so it results in  a much faster interaction  all the filters are automatically pushed  down and then the users also get a much  better  faster dashboard experience so finally  data studio passes that query into  bigquery which is the final query for  the dashboard which contains only the  data set  that this user is uh has access to c  and finally the results are back sent  back to data studio and  the history renders that data in  dashboard and the viewer finally can see  their dashboard with their own data so  since in the return query results step  only the users data set is passed there  is no way for  a different user to see that data set or  for this user to see data from a  different data set  so what's what does the end result look  like this is the whole process but when  the user finally opens the dashboard  they get a view that looks something  like this this is the view that all the  users  all the 1400 different partners see when  they open the dashboard they get a  dashboard like this  uh at the top there are filters they can  change the filters they can interact  with it and this dashboard will  indirectly present the data  for whatever filters or selections  they're making  each partner would see only their view  and not  they won't see anything else and based  on this dashboard  the team made some further improvements  where they added for their pages  they also added some monitoring where um  they could monitor  how the dashboard was being used through  strike stack driver  which partners were using it uh which  partners were uh  incurring additional uh data costs it's  also possible to set up  uh data limits by partner so  it's a future potential work is where we  could potentially take this  solution and then build in limits where  each partner would get 